Bro 👍 Here are 4 C# programs on classes and objects and 4 programs on method overloading. All programs accept input from the user, with no default input values.

# Part 1: Classes and Objects — 4 Programs

## 1. Student Details

Create a `Student` class with data members and a method to display student details.

```
using System;

class Student
{
    public int id;
    public string name;
    public string department;

    public void Display()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("ID: " + id);
        Console.WriteLine("Name: " + name);
        Console.WriteLine("Department: " + department);
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Student s = new Student();

        Console.Write("Enter ID: ");
        s.id = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());

        Console.Write("Enter Name: ");
        s.name = Console.ReadLine();

        Console.Write("Enter Department: ");
        s.department = Console.ReadLine();

        s.Display();
        Console.ReadLine();
    }
}
```

## 2. Employee Salary Calculation

Create an `Employee` class and calculate the annual salary.

```
using System;

class Employee
{
    public int id;
    public string name;
    public double salary;

    public void Display()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("ID: " + id);
        Console.WriteLine("Name: " + name);
        Console.WriteLine("Monthly Salary: " + salary);
        Console.WriteLine("Annual Salary: " + (salary * 12));
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Employee e = new Employee();

        Console.Write("Enter ID: ");
        e.id = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());

        Console.Write("Enter Name: ");
        e.name = Console.ReadLine();

        Console.Write("Enter Monthly Salary: ");
        e.salary = double.Parse(Console.ReadLine());

        e.Display();
        Console.ReadLine();
    }
}
```

